Three forces act on her:

- weight $mg=637$ N, vertically down;
- friction $f$ from the wall, vertically up (equal in length to $mg$);
- normal force $N$ from the wall, horizontally towards the centre.

No "centrifugal force" should be shown.

This is a low Earth orbit. Any two of:

- it is close to the surface, so high-resolution images can be taken (environmental monitoring, weather, surveillance);
- signals travel a short distance, giving low delay and requiring only low-power, compact transmitters and small antennas;
- less energy (cheaper) to launch than higher orbits;
- the short period lets it pass over large areas many times a day;
- it lies below the most intense parts of the Van Allen belts.

questions.question:
On the Hertzsprung–Russell diagram shown, describe where our Sun would be plotted at each of these times:

- X: before it joined the main sequence (while still contracting as a protostar);
- Y: 100 thousand years after it has left the main sequence;
- Z: 500 million years after it has completely exhausted its nuclear fuel.
